course: euphemisms
lesson: 11
title: Soft words for hard things: pregnancy and birth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grown-ups often talk about babies coming in soft, roundabout words instead of plain ones.
explain: Some soft words are polite or old-fashioned («a blessed event»), some are jokey slang («up the duff»), and some gently cover sad news («lost the baby»).
explain: The soft word is a kindness or a joke, but the plain meaning underneath is always the same.
explain: Vivid example: when Nan says «Auntie Jo has a little one on the way», she simply means Auntie Jo is pregnant and a baby is coming.
fact: "A blessed event" means the birth of a baby (old-fashioned, mostly US).
fact: "Up the duff" means pregnant (British slang, rather rude).
fact: "In the pudding club" means pregnant (British slang, jokey).
fact: "Preggers" means pregnant (British slang, chatty).
fact: "A little one on the way" means a baby is expected.
fact: "The patter of tiny feet" means a baby that is expected or wanted (jokey).
fact: "Bundle of joy" means a newborn baby.
fact: "Started a family" means had their first child.
fact: "Trying for a baby" means attempting to get pregnant.
fact: "Happy accident" means a baby that was not planned but is loved (jokey).
fact: "Change-of-life baby" means a baby born when the mother is near the end of the age for having children (old-fashioned).
fact: "Lost the baby" means had a miscarriage: the baby died before it could be born.
fact: "Pregnancy loss" is the official phrase for a miscarriage or a stillbirth (a baby that dies just before or while being born).
fact: "Termination" is the official word for an abortion, ending a pregnancy on purpose.
fact: "Baby bump" means a belly that clearly shows a pregnancy.
fact: "Showing" means the pregnancy can now be seen.
fact: "Confinement" means the time around giving birth (old-fashioned, mostly UK).
fact: "Love child" means a child whose parents were not married (old-fashioned).
fact: "Born out of wedlock" is the official phrase for born to parents who were not married.
fact: "Natural child" means a child born to unmarried parents (old-fashioned, UK).
exercise: What does "a blessed event" usually mean in an old-fashioned card? | The birth of a baby | A church festival | A lucky lottery win | A wedding in a church
exercise: What does "up the duff" mean in British slang? | Pregnant | Very full after dinner | In deep trouble | Covered in flour
exercise: What does "in the pudding club" mean when someone jokes about a woman? | She is pregnant | She joined a cooking group | She eats too many desserts | She won a baking prize
exercise: What does "preggers" mean in chatty British talk? | Pregnant | Grumpy | Very tired | Engaged to marry
exercise: Mum says, "There's a little one on the way." What does she mean? | A baby is expected | A small parcel is coming | A child is walking over | A puppy is being delivered
exercise: Grandad hopes to hear "the patter of tiny feet." What is he hoping for? | A baby in the family | Mice in the attic | Rain on the roof | A new pet kitten
exercise: What is "a bundle of joy" in a birth announcement? | A newborn baby | A big present | A parcel of sweets | A happy party guest
exercise: "They started a family last year." What happened? | They had their first child | They moved into a new house | They got married | They adopted a dog
exercise: What does "trying for a baby" mean? | Hoping and attempting to get pregnant | Babysitting for the neighbours every weekend | Looking after a doll | Choosing a baby name
exercise: Dad calls his youngest "our happy accident." What does he mean? | She was not planned but is loved | She falls over a lot | She was born in a hospital crash | She was found by chance
exercise: What is a "change-of-life baby"? | A baby born when the mother is near the end of her child-bearing years | A baby who changed everyone's habits and made the whole family get up early | A baby born after the family moved | A baby who was swapped at birth
exercise: "Sadly, she lost the baby." What happened? | The baby died before it was born | She forgot the baby somewhere | The baby got lost in a shop | The baby was given away
exercise: What does a doctor mean by "pregnancy loss"? | A miscarriage or a stillbirth | Losing weight while pregnant | A missed doctor's appointment | Losing a pregnancy test
exercise: What does "termination" mean in a medical leaflet about pregnancy? | An abortion, ending a pregnancy on purpose | The last stop at the end of a bus line | The birth of the baby | Being fired from a job
exercise: What is a "baby bump"? | A belly that shows a pregnancy | A bruise on a baby's head | A bump in a pram ride | A tiny speed bump
exercise: "She's not showing yet." What does that mean? | Her pregnancy cannot be seen yet | She has not done her play yet | She has not shown up yet | She is hiding her feelings
exercise: In an old novel, what was a lady's "confinement"? | The time around giving birth | A prison sentence for a crime she did | Being sent to her room | A long illness in bed
exercise: In an old story, what is a "love child"? | A child whose parents were not married | A very loving child who hugs everybody | A child adopted from abroad | A child who loves everyone
exercise: What does "born out of wedlock" mean on an old record? | Born to parents who were not married | Born outside a hospital, in a car or taxi | Born during a storm | Born away from home
exercise: In an old British history book, what is a "natural child"? | A child born to unmarried parents | A child born without a doctor | A child who loves nature | A child born at full term
test: News: "The couple are delighted to announce a blessed event in the spring." What will happen in spring? | They will have a baby | They will get married | They will win a prize | They will move to a church
test: Overheard on a bus: "She's up the duff again, that's number four." What is going on? | She is pregnant with her fourth child | She is in trouble for the fourth time | She ate four puddings | She has four dogs now
test: Text: "omg Sam's sister is preggers!!" What news is it? | Sam's sister is pregnant | Sam's sister is sick | Sam's sister is engaged | Sam's sister is angry
test: Gossip: "Rumour is she's in the pudding club, look at that bump." What does the gossip claim? | That she is pregnant | That she joined a baking club | That she ate too much pudding | That she hurt her tummy
test: A card says: "Congratulations on your bundle of joy!" What is the card for? | The birth of a baby | A new puppy in the house | A wedding | A birthday present
test: Mum whispers: "Don't ask Auntie about the baby, she lost it." What really happened? | Auntie's pregnancy ended and the baby died | Auntie forgot the baby at home on her own | The baby wandered off | Auntie gave the baby away
test: Dad laughs: "Your little brother? Happy accident, mate. Best one ever." What does Dad mean? | The brother was not planned but is loved | The brother was hurt in an accident | The brother is clumsy | The brother was found by chance
test: "We've been trying for a baby for two years." What do they want? | To have a baby | To adopt a puppy | To babysit more | To buy baby clothes
test: Old letter: "Mrs Gray is expecting her confinement in May." What will happen in May? | Mrs Gray will give birth | Mrs Gray will go to prison | Mrs Gray will stay in her room | Mrs Gray will move house
test: A politician says: "Every child, including those born out of wedlock, deserves equal rights." Which children are meant? | Children whose parents were not married | Children born abroad in another country | Children born at home | Children with no parents
test: Cousin: "She's 4 months gone and only just showing." What does "showing" mean here? | The pregnancy is starting to be seen | She is going on stage in the school show | She arrived at last | She is showing photos
test: Grandma: "Your gran was a change-of-life baby, her mum was 46." What does it tell you? | Her mother had her near the end of the child-bearing years | Gran changed her life at 46 | Gran was born late at night | Her mum changed jobs at 46
test: Leaflet: "Support is available after pregnancy loss." Who is the support for? | People whose baby died before or at birth | People who lost a lot of weight while ill | People who missed a check-up | People who lost a test result
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the expression "a little one on the way" when employed in respect of a family member. | A child is expected to be born | A small visitor is approaching | A minor parcel is in transit | A young child is walking towards the house
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the term "termination" in medical documentation pertaining to pregnancy. | The deliberate ending of a pregnancy | The natural birth of the infant | The conclusion of a hospital stay | The cessation of a doctor's employment
exam: It is stated that a couple "started a family" in the preceding year. Ascertain what is thereby communicated. | They had their first child | They were married | They acquired a residence | They adopted a domestic animal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "the patter of tiny feet"? | It refers to a baby that is expected or wished for | It describes rodents within a dwelling | It denotes the sound of rainfall | It signifies a dance performed by children
